  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Catalysing Collective and Principled Use of Data and Digital Technology in Humanitarian Action</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">IOM and partners will contribute to system-wide action in the critical first phases of the Humanitarian Data Collaborative through three key areas. We will contribute to the establishment of the global collaboration infrastructure of the Collaborative, as a member of the Steering Group convened under IASC auspices, contributing to its strategic direction, coherence across workstreams and liaison with the IASC Humanitarian Working Group, the Global IMWG, and the UN80 Data Quint. We will establish a shared evidence-base for action by conducting rapid diagnostics and facilitating the co-design of implementation plans for the Collaborative in two contexts. We will advance two high-impact opportunity areas: (a) defining and resourcing core data through a costed plan in the priority contexts, and (b) researching local data actor capacity and new models for collective data governance, across the priority contexts and beyond. Alongside these contributions to the Collaborative, and as a distinct output, IOM will also partner with the ICRC and UNHCR on an open-access publication setting a research and policy agenda for humanitarian action in a digital age, advancing the same Swiss priorities of digital foreign policy and digital public goods.</narrative>
  </description>
